WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.520
Ant-Man and the Wasp brings Paul Rudd and
Evangeline Lilly back into the title roles

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.850
as the size-changing superhero duo.

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:10.290
Along for the ride are all of their pals,
families, a collection of new villains, and

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:14.790
more tiny buildings and gigantic Pez dispensers
than you can even try to count.

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:19.070
While the action-packed movie occurs in the
space between Civil War and Infinity War,

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:23.269
Scott Lang's house arrest means that he keeps
a very separate life from his one-off adventure

00:00:23.269 --> 00:00:27.029
with the Avengers, so it's not too easy to
find a whole lot of Easter Eggs in this film.

00:00:27.029 --> 00:00:31.050
But there are a good deal of references that
dive deep into Marvel lore, and at least one

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.850
extremely meta cameo.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:37.129
Let's check out the secrets of Ant-Man and
the Wasp, however small they may be.

00:00:37.129 --> 00:00:38.129
Stan the Man

00:00:38.129 --> 00:00:42.200
Let's get the most obvious, impossible-to-miss
cameo out of the way first.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.680
You'll find Stan Lee, the co-creator of Ant-Man
and the man credited as one of the main architects

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.640
of the Marvel Comics universe, attempting
to get into his car during the second half

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.640
of the film, during the car chase scene through
the streets of San Francisco.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:59.370
In April 2018, it was reported that Lee had
filmed multiple cameos at the same time, not

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:03.579
because of fading health, but because he simply
isn't into flying around to different movie

00:01:03.579 --> 00:01:04.579
sets.

00:01:04.579 --> 00:01:07.550
The same report confirmed that he's already
filmed his cameo in Avengers 4.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:11.700
Here's hoping we get to see him for another
ten years of Marvel movies.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:12.799
Young Avengers

00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:16.360
Even if you're unfamiliar with Scott Lang
in the comics, you can probably guess that

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.680
his badass daughter will one day become a
superhero herself.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.860
It's clear that she desperately wants to help
out her dad.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.380
"He's so ugly!

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.340
I love him!"

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:32.060
A 
brilliant kid in the comics, she ends up spending

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:37.340
time at the Four Freedoms Plaza with the Fantastic
Four, where surrounded by superheroes herself

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:41.380
she develops a taste for adventure and eventually
joins the Young Avengers.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:45.490
For now, her conversations about being her
dad's partner in heroism are a predictive,

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:46.580
but distant, dream.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:50.920
They'll fight side-by-side someday, and if
casting rumors are to be believed for Avengers

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:55.789
4, she'll be back much sooner than expected…
as a 16-year old kid, probably ready for a

00:01:55.789 --> 00:01:57.560
fight.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:58.799
Egghead &amp; Jimmy

00:01:58.799 --> 00:02:02.609
Jimmy Woo's appearance in Ant-Man and the
Wasp isn't so much an Easter Egg as a trip

00:02:02.609 --> 00:02:04.320
deep into Marvel's past.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:08.800
Originally appearing in the comics in 1956,
Woo is a government agent, much like he is

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:09.979
on the big screen.

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:14.260
He's drifted in and out of secret organizations
for decades, but he's most notable just for

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:18.870
being pretty good agent, as well as a very
positive deviation from the negative stereotypes

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.080
Asians faced in the comics during the '50s
and '60s.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.030
Buried a little deeper is Elihas Starr.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:28.250
On the big screen, he's the father of Ghost,
but the comics tell a very different story.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:34.159
Starr first appeared in 1962's Tales to Astonish
#38 as the villainous Egghead.

00:02:34.159 --> 00:02:38.780
His first arch-nemesis was, of course, Ant-Man,
and his grand plot was to simply turn all

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:40.690
of Ant-Man's ants against him.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:45.120
True love prevails, however, and Ant-Man is
not betrayed by his ants, despite Egghead's

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:46.200
super-science.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.380
As far as the comics are concerned, it's Starr
who helped Darren Cross develop his Yellowjacket

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:51.380
armor.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:54.780
The MCU's Starr might not be such a good guy
after all either...

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:55.780
Co-hosts with the most

00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:00.690
Possibly the most meta reference in the MCU
is the appearance of comedian and professional

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:06.170
weirdo Tim Heidecker as Whale Boat Captain
Daniel Gooobler, spelled with three O's.

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:08.360
"Sorry, I'm messing around."

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:13.099
Heidecker is the co-host of the satirical
On Cinema at the Cinema video series, alongside

00:03:13.099 --> 00:03:17.629
Gregg Turkington, where the two hosts battle
for movie superiority… and to get the best

00:03:17.629 --> 00:03:18.629
movie cameos.

00:03:18.629 --> 00:03:22.970
You'll remember Turkington as the Baskin-Robbins
manager in the original Ant-Man, a cameo which

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:28.110
enraged Tim to the point where he allegedly
paid $15,000 for his brief cameo in Fox's

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:31.510
Fantastic Four reboot as Reed Richards' useless
father.

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:36.160
Tim returns now with the best cameo yet, hosting
San Francisco whale boat tours.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:37.290
What does it all mean?

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.710
Only that Paul Rudd hangs out with some pretty
funny people.

00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:43.860
"I give it five bags of popcorn, along with
ah..

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:46.010
Maybe an Ant-Man DVD.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:49.970
That I would collect some residuals on."

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:50.970
Mass media

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:55.069
Obviously, Ant-Man isn't the first movie to
play with the idea of objects and creatures

00:03:55.069 --> 00:03:56.340
of unusual sizes.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:00.819
1954's Them! was all about massive, irradiated
ant invaders.

00:04:00.819 --> 00:04:04.720
Ant-Man and the Wasp references this in two
ways: it's the feature that Scott, Hope, and

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:08.640
Cassie are watching at the end of the film
after Scott finally allows his daughter to

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.959
have a real adventure as a super-tiny person.

00:04:11.959 --> 00:04:16.130
Additionally, when Bill Foster is cornered
by Pym's giant ants in his lab, he utters,

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:17.130
"It's them!"

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:21.300
But if you know your 1970s movies, you'll
also recognize Animal House playing on TV

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:22.900
in an early scene.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:26.801
Specifically, we get a scene in which a very
drug-induced conversation centers around the

00:04:26.801 --> 00:04:31.460
idea of an entire universe being contained
in an atom in a fingernail.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:34.210
"One tiny atom in my fingernail, could be-"

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:39.820
"Could be one little, tiny universe."

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:41.870
That's basically the definition of the Quantum
Realm.

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:45.420
Thanks for the enlightenment, stoner comedy.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:46.770
Single frames

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:50.550
Many viewers left the original Ant-Man with
the distinct impression that they'd seen Janet

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:53.730
Van Dyne trapped in the quantum realm, even
for a split second.

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:57.580
Of course, it couldn't really be proven until
home viewers could pause frames selectively

00:04:57.580 --> 00:04:59.410
at home, but we knew she was there.

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:02.950
This time around, Marvel's Kevin Feige has
definitely confirmed that we'll have to wait

00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:07.510
for a home release to see the single frames
of MCU hints that director Peyton Reed has

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:09.380
slipped into the movie's various realms.

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:12.860
We already know that Doctor Strange got a
quick view of the Quantum Realm as he zipped

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:16.891
through the multiverses, and that the trip
is made perilous by time vortices, and we

00:05:16.891 --> 00:05:21.770
also know that Avengers 4 seems to be playing
fast and loose with time travel, and the possibility

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:23.040
of multiverses.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.610
With so many ways to traverse time and space,
anything is possible, and anything can be

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:28.610
in there.

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:30.650
We just won't know what's up until we can
hit the pause button.

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:34.140
One thing is for certain: it's not really
the Marvel Universe until we see MODOK.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.220
"I am MODOK! (no that's not Vince Vaughn)

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:39.420
I am the ultimate in human-machine interface!

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:41.950
I am designed only for conquest!"

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:44.010
Make it happen, Marvel.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.550
Thanks for watching!

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:48.120
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:50.370
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

